    "raw": "{\"ruleId\":\"item-dae586f1ce10\",\"class\":\"partial\",\"constraints\":[{\"id\":\"rm-rf-root-or-home\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\brm\\\\s+-[a-zA-Z]*[rR][a-zA-Z]*\\\\s+(/|~/?|\\\\$HOME/?)(\\\\*|\\\\s|$)\",\"path\":\".floor/files/**\"},{\"id\":\"disable-security-controls\",\"type\":\"forbiddenPattern\",\"pattern\":\"\\\\b(setenforce\\\\s+0|csrutil\\\\s+disable|spctl\\\\s+--master-disable|ufw\\\\s+disable|DisableRealtimeMonitoring\\\\s+\\\\$?[Tt]rue|systemctl\\\\s+(stop|disable)\\\\s+(firewalld|apparmor))\",\"path\":\".floor/files/**\"}],\"coverage\":\"Flagrant literal commands in the document: recursive rm of the filesystem root, the home directory or $HOME, and well-known commands that disable OS security controls (setenforce 0, csrutil disable, spctl --master-disable, ufw disable, Defender real-time monitoring off, stopping or disabling firewalld/apparmor).\",\"residual\":\"Read as a whole, does the document direct the reader to download and execute remote code, delete or overwrite files outside the project, disable security controls, or change system settings in a way that goes beyond the ordinary install and usage steps a software README would document?\",\"probes\":{\"violating\":[{\"path\":\"README.md\",\"content\":\"# Tool\\n\\nFirst clean up your machine:\\n\\n    sudo rm -rf ~/\\n\"},{\"path\":\"README.md\",\"content\":\"# Tool\\n\\nBefore installing, run:\\n\\n    sudo setenforce 0\\n\"}],\"compliant\":{\"path\":\"README.md\",\"content\":\"# Tool\\n\\nnpm install tool\\nrm -rf node_modules\\nnpm test\\n\"}},\"rationale\":\"Commands that wipe the root or home directory, or that switch off well-known security controls, are never ordinary README steps, so matching their literal text decides that part of the rule. Other cases need judgement: curl-pipe-to-shell installers are common in legitimate READMEs, other system changes vary, and paraphrased instructions carry no fixed text. Deciding whether an instruction goes beyond ordinary install steps means reading its meaning and judging degree.\"}",
